      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Yes, mindfulness exercises during meals could help. Also, is stomach acid reflux related to psychological health or stress?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is stomach acid reflux related to psychological health?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To practice mindfulness during meals, try these exercises: eat slowly and chew thoroughly, put your utensils down between bites, and focus on the taste, texture, and aroma of your food. Avoid distractions like your phone or TV, and take a few deep breaths before starting your meal to center your attention. These habits help you enjoy your food more and recognize fullness cues [clueweb22-en0002-65-07759:12]. Regarding acid reflux, research shows that stress and anxiety can worsen symptoms by affecting gut motility, increasing sensitivity to pain, and even altering the function of the lower esophageal sphincter. Chronic stress may also lead to inflammation and delayed gastric emptying, both of which can trigger or exacerbate acid reflux. The relationship is often bidirectional: stress can cause reflux, and persistent reflux can increase anxiety or depression [clueweb22-en0003-88-05119:7][clueweb22-en0015-68-05661:4][clueweb22-en0002-65-07759:12].",
