          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, but I\u2019m more interested in the link between psychological health and digestion. Is stomach acid reflux related to psychological health?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is stomach acid reflux related to psychological health?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Research shows a connection between psychological health and acid reflux. Anxiety and stress don't always directly cause acid reflux, but they can increase your sensitivity to heartburn pain and may worsen symptoms if you already have conditions like GERD (Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease) [clueweb22-en0034-41-14447][clueweb22-en0003-88-05119]. Muscle tension from anxiety can increase stomach pressure, potentially pushing acid upward and causing heartburn. Additionally, the discomfort of frequent acid reflux can itself become a source of stress or anxiety, creating a cycle where each can exacerbate the other [clueweb22-en0003-88-05119]. Managing stress through mindfulness, exercise, or relaxation techniques may help relieve both psychological and digestive symptoms. Are there specific foods that can help reduce acid reflux symptoms?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0034-41-14447:2": 0.04761211785223427,
            "clueweb22-en0037-51-11247:12": 0.045807473674401265,
