          "user_utterance": "Those are great suggestions! Can you tell me how psychological health might relate to stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is stomach acid reflux related to psychological health?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Psychological health and acid reflux (GERD) are closely linked\u2014each can worsen the other:\n\n- **Stress, Anxiety, and Depression Can Trigger or Worsen Acid Reflux:** High stress and anxiety increase sensitivity to reflux symptoms, even if actual acid levels don\u2019t rise. Stress may also make the esophagus more vulnerable to acid, leading to more discomfort ([clueweb22-en0002-65-07759], [clueweb22-en0008-04-03010]).\n- **Acid Reflux Can Impact Mental Health:** Persistent reflux symptoms can lead to or worsen anxiety and depression. Studies show a bidirectional relationship\u2014people with depression are more likely to develop GERD, and vice versa ([clueweb22-en0002-65-07759]).\n- **Quality of Life:** GERD patients with anxiety or depression often experience more severe symptoms and lower quality of life, even if their reflux is not objectively worse.\n- **Managing Both:** Addressing stress (e.g., through exercise, mindfulness, or therapy) can reduce reflux symptoms. Treating reflux",
