          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Eating dinner late at night can indeed worsen acid reflux symptoms, as it can cause stomach acid to flow back up into the esophagus while you're sleeping. Additionally, late-night eating can also lead to weight gain, as your body's metabolism slows down during sleep, causing the calories you consume to be stored as fat. To minimize these risks, consider eating dinner earlier, allowing for a gap of 11 to 12 hours between dinner and breakfast, and giving your body time to digest food properly before sleeping. This can help improve digestion, reduce acid reflux, and support weight loss.   Please go ahead and provide the next conversation. I'm ready when you are.",
