  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini-nopersonal",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "1-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I want to start living healthier and be more active, but not sure where to begin. Any simple tips?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to get started to have a healthier and more active lifestyle?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Great \u2014 keep it simple and start with tiny, consistent changes. Try this starter plan:  - Pick one motivating goal (feel more energetic, lose a little weight, reduce stress). - Move daily: walk 20\u201330 minutes or break activity into 3\u20135 minute micro-workouts throughout the day. - Do 2\u20133 short strength sessions per week (bodyweight moves: squats, push-ups, planks, lunges). Vary exercises rather than just adding weight to get better results. - Swap one meal a day for whole, real foods (vegetables, lean protein, whole grains). Aim for simple 30-minute dinners and plan a basic frugal pantry. - Declutter one small area (desk or kitchen) to reduce mental clutter and make healthy habits easier. - Use small, progressive steps: add 1 more minute, 1 extra rep, or 1 healthy swap each week. - Get accountability: a friend, online challenge, or habit tracker helps stick to it.  If you want, I can build a 2-week beginner plan (workouts + meal swaps) tailored to your schedule.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0043-65-19029:3": 24.48040008544922,
            "clueweb22-en0042-11-19536:0": 24.231800079345703,
            "clueweb22-en0010-58-00115:0": 24.21339988708496,
            "clueweb22-en0032-59-07821:14": 22.89900016784668,
            "clueweb22-en0033-08-13734:15": 22.547100067138672
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s super helpful! I like the idea of micro-workouts and simple dinner swaps. I eat dinner really late\u2014would shifting that earlier help, or should I just focus on healthy options at any time?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some practical steps to have a healthier and more active lifestyle?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Shifting dinner earlier usually helps, but both timing and what you eat matter \u2014 so do whichever you can change first.  Why earlier helps - Finishing 2\u20133 hours before bed improves digestion, reduces reflux, and can help sleep and metabolism.   - Aiming for ~11\u201312 hours from dinner to breakfast (e.g., finish at 8:30 p.m., eat breakfast ~8:30 a.m.) naturally limits late-night snacking.  If you can\u2019t eat earlier - Focus on lighter, healthy dinners (lean protein, vegetables, whole grains) and avoid big, heavy or very carb-heavy meals right before bed.   - Split your meal if you work out after dinner: eat carbs earlier for energy and protein/veg after.   - Pack a healthy dinner or keep simple options at work so you\u2019re not forced to eat late.  Practical start: move dinner 15\u201330 minutes earlier each week until you hit your target, and combine with your micro-workouts and one daily whole-food swap.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0037-52-07852:0": 52.64820098876953,
            "clueweb22-en0003-51-13810:9": 49.62820053100586,
            "clueweb22-en0046-31-19033:7": 49.4556999206543,
            "clueweb22-en0028-10-01926:8": 47.32469940185547,
            "clueweb22-en0045-01-06850:1": 46.657100677490234
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s practical, thanks! I usually eat out, mostly pizza from a local place and sometimes heavy stuff. Any smart tricks to make eating out healthier, especially for protein?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some practical steps to have a healthier and more active lifestyle?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Great \u2014 practical, tasty ideas you can use right away:  - Pick the protein first: order grilled/steamed/roasted proteins (chicken breast, turkey, fish, tofu, shrimp) rather than fried or breaded. - Ask for sauces on the side and \u201clight oil\u201d or \u201cno added salt\u201d to cut calories and sodium. - Double up on veggies: swap fries/rice for extra veggies or a side salad. - For pizza: choose thin crust, tomato base, lean toppings (grilled chicken, veggies), and skip extra cheese or ask for less. - Share or portion control: split an entr\u00e9e or box half before you eat so you don\u2019t overeat. - Lean swaps: brown rice/quinoa instead of white rice or noodles when possible. - Time it: eat a small veggie or piece of fruit before you go out to reduce overeating. - Bring a backup protein: a protein bar or a small cooked chicken breast works if options are limited. - Simple rule: if the dish is mostly protein + veggies with a light sauce, it\u2019s usually the healthier pick.  Want quick sample orders for pizza, Chinese, or fast-casual places?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0046-38-08098:1": 30.574399948120117,
            "clueweb22-en0004-90-11771:15": 28.902099609375,
            "clueweb22-en0043-38-18761:2": 28.49169921875,
            "clueweb22-en0041-02-11766:5": 28.28059959411621,
            "clueweb22-en0031-68-13523:6": 28.0935001373291
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Yes, sample \u201chealthier\u201d orders for pizza and Chinese would be great! Oh, and if you have a simple protein snack idea for work, that\u2019d help too.",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some practical steps to have a have better psychological health?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "Great \u2014 quick, practical picks:  Pizza - Thin crust with tomato base, ask for light cheese or half the usual amount.   - Lean toppings: grilled chicken, shrimp, mushrooms, spinach, peppers, onions.   - Skip pepperoni/extra cheese; add a side salad or roasted veg instead.   - Ask the kitchen to box half before you start or portion off a serving.  Chinese - Pick steamed/grilled proteins or stir-fries with lots of vegetables (chicken, shrimp, lean pork, tofu).   - Ask for sauces on the side and \u201clight oil/no added salt.\u201d Favor ginger, hoisin, plum or bean-style sauces.   - Choose steamed or boiled rice (or half a portion) instead of egg fried rice; avoid prawn crackers and heavy fried starters.   - Better starters: clear broth soup, steamed vegetables, or a simple salad. Use chopsticks to slow eating.  Portable protein snack for work (easy) - Cooked chicken breast slices or canned tuna + a few whole-grain crackers and cherry tomatoes.   - Or 2 hard\u2011boiled eggs + a small handful of almonds \u2014 portable, filling, and high in protein.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0046-38-08098:1": 32.882301330566406,
