      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "This is exactly the sort of advice I needed! Going to try those combos. \n\nI\u2019ve heard a lot about \u201cmental health\u201d being tied to lifestyle too. What are some practical steps for better psychological health?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some practical steps to have a have better psychological health?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Nice \u2014 glad it helps! Here are simple, practical lifestyle steps you can start this week to boost your mental health:  - Move daily: keep your 20\u201330 min walk or micro\u2011workouts \u2014 activity reliably improves mood.   - Prioritize sleep: aim for consistent bed/wake times, finish eating 2\u20133 hours before bed, and cut screens 30\u201360 min before lights out.   - Brief mindfulness: 5 minutes of breathing or a short guided meditation each day (box breath: inhale 4s, hold 4s, exhale 4s).   - Small wins: set one tiny daily goal (make a healthy dinner, tidy your desk 10\u201315 min) and check it off.   - Gratitude/journaling: nightly list of 2\u20133 things you\u2019re grateful for or one positive moment from the day.   - Social connection: schedule one short check\u2011in a week with a friend or colleague.   - Food and routine: keep protein + veggies at meals, hydrate, limit alcohol and excess caffeine.   - Nature and breaks: get outside for even 10 minutes midday.   - Ask for help if needed: if low mood or anxiety persist or affect daily life, reach out to a GP or mental\u2011health professional.  Small, consistent changes compound \u2014 combine a few and build gradually.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0011-04-02143:4": 29.91510009765625,
            "clueweb22-en0011-04-02143:3": 29.724300384521484,
            "clueweb22-en0034-12-19627:1": 29.050100326538086,
            "clueweb22-en0034-12-19627:0": 28.943300247192383,
            "clueweb22-en0040-70-03476:0": 26.817899703979492
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thank you! Speaking of health and food, is stomach acid reflux related to psychological health or stress?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is stomach acid reflux related to psychological health?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Short answer: yes \u2014 stress and mental\u2011health issues are linked with acid reflux, but the relationship is complex and works both ways.  What happens - Stress and anxiety can make reflux feel worse by increasing your sensitivity to stomach acid and by changing behaviors (late or heavy eating, alcohol, smoking) that trigger reflux.   - Studies also show a two\u2011way association: people with GERD are more likely to have anxiety or depression, and those conditions can make reflux symptoms worse.  Practical steps - Reduce stress: daily movement, 5 minutes of breathing/meditation, and regular sleep.   - Eat/timing: finish meals 2\u20133 hours before bed, choose lighter dinners, and avoid big late\u2011night carbs or alcohol.   - Habit fixes: cut smoking/alcohol, avoid overeating, and practice relaxation (yoga, walks).   - Get help: treating reflux (diet, meds, doctor) and addressing anxiety/depression (therapy, GP) often improves both.  If reflux is frequent or affecting sleep/breathing, see your clinician for evaluation.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0032-63-09386:4": 64.51349639892578,
            "clueweb22-en0002-65-07759:15": 61.73109817504883,
            "clueweb22-en0032-63-09386:2": 57.0364990234375,
