    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s super helpful! I like the idea of micro-workouts and simple dinner swaps. I eat dinner really late\u2014would shifting that earlier help, or should I just focus on healthy options at any time?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some practical steps to have a healthier and more active lifestyle?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Shifting dinner earlier usually helps, but both timing and what you eat matter \u2014 so do whichever you can change first.  Why earlier helps - Finishing 2\u20133 hours before bed improves digestion, reduces reflux, and can help sleep and metabolism.   - Aiming for ~11\u201312 hours from dinner to breakfast (e.g., finish at 8:30 p.m., eat breakfast ~8:30 a.m.) naturally limits late-night snacking.  If you can\u2019t eat earlier - Focus on lighter, healthy dinners (lean protein, vegetables, whole grains) and avoid big, heavy or very carb-heavy meals right before bed.   - Split your meal if you work out after dinner: eat carbs earlier for energy and protein/veg after.   - Pack a healthy dinner or keep simple options at work so you\u2019re not forced to eat late.  Practical start: move dinner 15\u201330 minutes earlier each week until you hit your target, and combine with your micro-workouts and one daily whole-food swap.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0037-52-07852:0": 52.64820098876953,
            "clueweb22-en0003-51-13810:9": 49.62820053100586,
            "clueweb22-en0046-31-19033:7": 49.4556999206543,
