      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thank you! Speaking of health and food, is stomach acid reflux related to psychological health or stress?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is stomach acid reflux related to psychological health?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Short answer: yes \u2014 stress and mental\u2011health issues are linked with acid reflux, but the relationship is complex and works both ways.  What happens - Stress and anxiety can make reflux feel worse by increasing your sensitivity to stomach acid and by changing behaviors (late or heavy eating, alcohol, smoking) that trigger reflux.   - Studies also show a two\u2011way association: people with GERD are more likely to have anxiety or depression, and those conditions can make reflux symptoms worse.  Practical steps - Reduce stress: daily movement, 5 minutes of breathing/meditation, and regular sleep.   - Eat/timing: finish meals 2\u20133 hours before bed, choose lighter dinners, and avoid big late\u2011night carbs or alcohol.   - Habit fixes: cut smoking/alcohol, avoid overeating, and practice relaxation (yoga, walks).   - Get help: treating reflux (diet, meds, doctor) and addressing anxiety/depression (therapy, GP) often improves both.  If reflux is frequent or affecting sleep/breathing, see your clinician for evaluation.",
